The test scenario:

Goal: Arrow down from "Text message" (#reminder-2) to opt-out answer (#reminder-none) should hide the mobile number field (#conditional-reminder-2).

Observed failure: At step index 2 (the second ArrowDown press), after moving focus to #reminder-none, assertion expects visible selector "#conditional-reminder-2" false but it's true (still visible). So the conditional block remains visible when selecting none.

Looking at the DOM structure:

There are two groups of radios:
- First group inside div[data-module="govuk-radios"] id="channel-radios". Contains three radios? Actually first: email (with data-aria-controls="conditional-reminder"), second: text (with data-aria-controls="conditional-reminder-2").
- Then divider "or".
- Second group: optout radios with id="optout-radios", containing radio #reminder-none (no data-aria-controls attribute).

Thus the radios module likely processes inputs within the same $module element (i.e., the element passed to Radios constructor). In the script, they call document.querySelectorAll('[data-module="govuk-radios"]') which selects both divs: channel-radios and optout-radios. Each gets its own Radios instance.

Now, the issue: When focusing on #reminder-none (which belongs to optout-radios module), pressing ArrowDown moves focus to next radio in DOM order? Actually arrow key navigation among radios typically works via roving tabindex or using native radio group behavior: arrow keys move focus within same radiogroup (same name). Since all radios share name="reminder", they are in same radiogroup across both modules. ArrowDown from #reminder-2 goes to #reminder-none (next in DOM). That triggers a change event? Actually selecting a radio via keyboard updates its checked property and triggers change event. The Radios module listens for click events only, not change events. It sets aria-expanded and toggles conditional visibility based on click handling. However, when a radio is changed via keyboard, the click listener does not fire (since no click). Thus the conditional visibility is not updated.

In the observed logs: before second ArrowDown, #reminder-2 had aria-expanded="true" and aria-controls="conditional-reminder-2". After moving to #reminder-none, we see that #reminder-none has no aria-controls attribute (since it never had data-aria-controls). The conditional-reminder-2 remains visible because its class govuk-radios__conditional--hidden was not toggled (since the radio that controls it, #reminder-2, became unchecked but its setAttributes wasn't called). Indeed, the Radios.prototype.setAttributes is only called on click (handleClick) and during init (when setting attributes based on initial checked state). It doesn't respond to change events.

